<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denis of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1261">1261</a>–<a href="/years/1325">1325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Dinis of Portugal, Denis the Farmer King · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denis (Dinis) was king of Portugal from 1279 to 1325. He fixed the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcanices">Treaty of Alcañices</a> in 1297, founded the university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1290, made Portuguese the language of government and founded the Order of Christ in 1319 to take over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>' property. Known as the Farmer King, he encouraged agriculture and planted the pine forest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiria">Leiria</a>, and was himself a noted poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denis-of-portugal.jpg" alt="Denis of Portugal" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D._Dinis_-_Compendio_de_cr%C3%B3nicas_de_reyes_(Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Denis was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 9 October 1261, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-iii-of-portugal">Afonso III of Portugal</a> and Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, an illegitimate daughter of King Afonso X of Castile. The marriage, made around 1253, settled a dispute over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, but Afonso was still married to Matilda II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, who accused him of bigamy before Pope Alexander IV in 1255. The pope condemned Afonso III for adultery in 1258 and placed him under interdict, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> legitimised Denis and his siblings in 1263. Denis married Elizabeth of Aragon, who was later canonised, and as heir he shared government with his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Denis came to the throne in 1279 and reigned for over 46 years. In 1284 he opened inquiries into the expropriation of royal property, to the church's detriment, and the next year he promulgated amortisation laws that prohibited the church and religious orders from buying land. He also persuaded Pope Nicholas IV in 1288 to separate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> in Portugal from the one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and in 1289 he signed the Concordat of Forty Articles with the pope. He went to war with Castile in 1295, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcanices">Treaty of Alcañices</a> two years later brought forty years of peace.</p><p>He created numerous towns and trade fairs, ordered many castles built, and set up the Bolsa de Comércio, approved on 10 May 1293, the first documented form of marine insurance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1308 he signed the first Portuguese commercial agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in 1317 he hired the Genoese sailor Manuel Pessanha as hereditary admiral, effectively founding the Portuguese Navy. He also ordered the pine forest near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiria">Leiria</a> planted to protect farmland from advancing coastal sands and to supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The last years were troubled by rivalry between his legitimate heir Afonso and his illegitimate son Afonso Sanches, which led to civil war in 1322-1324. Queen Elizabeth acted as intermediary, and by legend rode out between the armies at Alvalade in 1323. Denis died at Santarém on 7 January 1325 and was buried at Odivelas.</p><p>He wrote lyric poetry in the troubadour tradition, and 137 songs attributed to him survive, including his love songs, the Cantigas de Amigo. Contemporary chronicles describe him as wise and able, though he is also described as sometimes cruel in family relations. The historian Duarte Nunes de Leão first called him father of his country in 1600.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Denis of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>King Denis</b> (r. 1279–1325) encouraged farming and trade, founded a university in Lisbon in 1290, later moved to Coimbra, and promoted Portuguese as the language of royal documents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-portugal">Santarém</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1302" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Denis of Portugal"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 4234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 4234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1302">What was here in 1302? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
